Laura (docs): Heads-up: I opened a short PR to add a Node.js streaming example for audit-log exports and a compact redaction checklist for SOC2 reviewers. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/2512

Tom (security): Nice. Please include exact default sensitive keys we redact server-side (ip, email, api_key, auth_token, location). Also note hashed identifiers allowed if customer requests.

Jules (eng): Added a draft Node snippet to the PR showing client stream download + incremental SHA256 verification. Uses the new header `X-Redwood-Export-Version: 2`.

Laura (docs): Can you paste the snippet here so I can copy into the article?

Jules (eng): Sure: ```js
const res = await fetch('https://export.redwood.ai/v2/exports/exp_2025_02', {
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${TOKEN}`, 'X-Redwood-Export-Version': '2' }
});
const stream = res.body;
const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256');
for await (const chunk of stream) {
  hash.update(chunk);
  // write chunk to file or process events
}
console.log('sha256:', hash.digest('hex'))
```

Sam (devex): Love that. Add a one-liner showing how to request a "redaction-first" export vs a "full-export" (enterprise).

Laura (docs): I'll add both. Also adding a tiny checklist at the top: 1) confirm server-side PII redaction; 2) require consent record for full exports; 3) include integrity checksum; 4) retain purge logs for proof.

Priya (legal): For full exports, legal needs `consent_id` and `consent_ts` stored in export metadata, plus the identity of the requester. Add wording about record retention obligations by region.

Tom (security): Add sentence: "Do not rely on client-side redaction; server-side enforcement is authoritative." Also recommend TLS + at-rest AES-256 and SHA256 checksum verification.

Laura (docs): Great — I'll update the PR, include the Node snippet, the checklist, consent fields, and a short migration note for `user_id` -> `actor.id`. Requesting review from Priya & Tom.

CI-bot: docs/pr-2512  checks passed :white_check_mark:

Laura (docs): Thanks everyone — plan to merge tomorrow if no objections.
